{"kind":"report","text":"APPROVED: exact head 7b43035c4c2006f3b647c95498cadc6457494a6d, all four artifacts, for implementation request #24725 and review request #24731.\n\nArchitecture: layers 6 (projections and queries) and 7 (CLI and MCP surfaces) are affected. The host's existing verified Snapshot and ReadOnlySnapshot contracts remain intact. One private builder computes the plan; Build uses ReadOnlySnapshot for diagnostics and BuildForLanding uses Snapshot for writing commands. The architecture change is documented and published in docs/reference/architecture.md@7b43035c4c2006f3b647c95498cadc6457494a6d. The new frontier_allowed reason is truthful for both builders. The read-only preview wording in docs/reference/gs/land.md@7b43035c4c2006f3b647c95498cadc6457494a6d describes its lack of staging and reservation; it does not promise that a writing command leaves local acceleration state untouched. The explicit no-acceleration-mutation contracts of gs merge-plan and the MCP merge_plan tool remain enforced.\n\nSecurity: the cached path still checks the current sequence head and selected projection profile, verifies authenticated history on a cache miss, and preserves rollback-witness checks. The merge still revalidates approval, implementer, destination and authorization, compares the current frontier with the planned frontier before landing, and advances the sealed target by compare-and-swap. No signature, secret, input-bound, receipt or authority checks are removed. This is a direct implementation commission, not follow-on work claiming adoption through an authority-bearing decision-artifact chain. No unresolved security defect found.\n\nSimplification: sharing one private builder avoids duplicated plan logic and limits the change to choosing the snapshot path. The existing plan seam remains useful for call-site regression tests; its pointer check binds the seam to the cached builder, while the separate snapshot-path test proves the behavior. No unnecessary production mechanism or scope found.\n\nEvidence: independently ran the three new landing tests and the CLI diagnostic immutability test together, plus the existing app snapshot-cache, read-only snapshot and concurrent append/witness tests. All passed. Independently ran go test ./... -count=1 -timeout 40m at this exact head; all packages passed. git diff --check passed.\n\nSix independent, compiling mutations each failed in the named committed test: cached builder changed to ReadOnlySnapshot (TestALandingPlanSharesTheWorkspaceFold); land preview bypassed the seam for Build (TestLandBuildsBothPlansThroughTheLandingSeam); merge plan bypassed the seam for Build (TestMergeBuildsItsPlanThroughTheLandingSeam); seam bound to Build (the same merge seam test); CLI diagnostic changed to BuildForLanding (TestMergePlanAllowedResultLeavesGitAndWorkroomAccelerationStateUnchanged); MCP diagnostic changed to BuildForLanding (TestMergePlanToolAllowedResultLeavesGitAndWorkroomAccelerationStateUnchanged). Every mutation was restored, and the temporary review worktree and branch were removed.\n\nThe reporting artifact #24730 explicitly limits its artroom figures to replaying the three snapshot reads: 70.9 s before and 23.1 s after, with gs merge-plan unchanged at 24.6 s. I inspected that harness and confirmed its stated limitation: the plans refuse after the fold, so this is not a complete landing measurement. I did not independently repeat those wall-time measurements. The deterministic fold-count and equality tests substantiate the optimization; the request permits an explicit statement of what was not measured. Conditions 1-6 are met.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:4677007c599cc3028e6ceea4fa95c1eaa934ab7c","binding":"assigned","head":"7b43035c4c2006f3b647c95498cadc6457494a6d","head_news_acknowledged":"[\"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:ef6323c5d62626d61395905fca18983807d932b2\"]","implementations":"[\"git:sha1:5d262